    </ul>
</nav>

<h2 id="editor">The editor</h2>
<p>The editor is at <a href="/edit">/edit</a>. The page list has a form to create or open a page by
its path, and every live and deleted page has its own editor at <code>/edit/&lt;path&gt;</code>.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Save</strong> compiles the template and stores the source and the compiled image.
    A page that does not compile is still stored, so you do not lose your work.</li>
    <li><strong>Save and view</strong> saves, then opens the page. If it does not compile, you stay
    in the editor with the diagnostics instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnostics</strong> name the line and column in your template. A diagnostic that
    has a position is a link to the matching number in the line gutter.</li>
    <li><strong>State</strong> is what the page's stored image amounts to: <em>Compiled</em>,
    <em>Compiled with warnings</em>, <em>Compile failed</em>, <em>Not compiled</em>, or <em>Stale</em>
    when the image was built by an earlier deploy. A page compiled elsewhere says <em>Stale</em>
    until it is next requested, which recompiles it.</li>
    <li><strong>History</strong> lists the saved revisions of a page. Choosing one shows its stored
    source without compiling it.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete</strong> hides a page but keeps its template and history. <strong>Scrub</strong>
    permanently removes a deleted page and its retained history; use it only when you mean it.</li>
    <li><strong>Restore from seed</strong> is offered for the pages the site ships with, such as
    <code>/_layout</code>, <code>/help</code> and the stylesheets. It saves the bundled source as a
    new revision.</li>
    <li>The assistant is available from the editor only when the host enables it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Paths are canonical: <code>/About</code>, <code>/about/</code> and <code>/about</code> are the
same page. A path the site's own routes already answer for, such as <code>/edit</code> or
<code>/login</code>, cannot be created as a page.</p>

<h2 id="fragment">A page is a fragment</h2>
<p>Every HTML page is wrapped in the site's default layout, <code>/_layout</code>. The layout writes
the document: the doctype, <code>html</code>, <code>head</code>, title, stylesheets, the site menu,
the banner, the <code>main</code> landmark and the close. Your page writes only what belongs inside
<code>main</code>.</p>
<p>Do not write a doctype, <code>html</code>, <code>head</code>, <code>body</code>, a
<code>title</code> element or the site menu. Start at the page's first heading and write one
<code>h1</code>.</p>
<p>A response that is not HTML, a redirect and the empty body of a <code>HEAD</code> response are not
wrapped. A page whose key has a content type of its own, such as <code>/site.css</code> or
<code>/editor.js</code>, is served as that content type and is never wrapped.</p>

<h2 id="layout">Layout and title</h2>
<p>The layout slots are reached through the page's features, so a page that never wants a layout
never meets one:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% IPageLayout layout = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IPageLayout&gt;();
   layout.Title = "About";
   layout.AddStylesheet("/about.css"); %&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;About&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This page is wrapped in the site layout.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
<p><strong>A wrapped page must set a title.</strong> Reading <code>Title</code> before any layer sets
it throws, and the host refuses the chain before the layout runs, so the error names the page that
forgot rather than the layout that noticed. A layout may read the title so far and compose its own:
<code>layout.Title = $"{layout.Title} — Site";</code>.</p>
<p><code>AddStylesheet("/about.css")</code> links one sheet. Call it zero, one or many times. The
document's base sheets come first, then each layout's from the outermost in, then the page's in the
order it asked. A sheet must be a site-relative URL, and an exact duplicate is linked once.</p>
<p><code>AddHead("&lt;meta name=&quot;robots&quot; content=&quot;noindex&quot;&gt;")</code> adds raw markup to the
document's <code>head</code>. Use it for a meta tag or another document-level link, not for a
stylesheet; <code>AddStylesheet</code> owns those.</p>
<p><code>layout.Layout</code> names another stored page to wrap this one. The host pre-fills it with
the site default, so leaving it alone gets the site chrome. Setting it to <code>null</code> serves
the page alone: do that only when the page genuinely writes its own whole document, because nothing
else will give it a head, a title or a menu.</p>
<p>Inside a layout, <code>layout.Body</code> is the page the layout wraps. It is empty in the content
layer, because only a layout has something to wrap.</p>
<p>The chain renders inside out: the page first, then its layout, then the layout's parent. The
whole chain shares one render budget, one fetch allowance and one body allowance, so two layers do
not get two bites. The default chain is limited to four layouts, and the host stops at the first
repeat, so a layout cannot wrap itself forever.</p>
<p>If the default layout is missing or does not compile, the page is served unwrapped and the host
logs a warning. A layout the page named that cannot be served is an error that names the missing
layout.</p>
<p>A layout is not a guard. The content has already run by the time the layout starts, so a layout
can hide chrome but cannot prevent a side effect.</p>
<p>A minimal layout writes the document through the shared builder and puts the page in the middle:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% var layout = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IPageLayout&gt;();
   if (ctx.Path == ctx.Request.Path) { layout.Title = "Site"; }
   bool At(string href) =&gt; ctx.Request.Path == href;
   var links = new System.Collections.Generic.List&lt;NavLink&gt; { new("/", "Home", At("/")) }; %&gt;
&lt;%== PageDocument.Head(layout.Title, layout.Stylesheets, head: layout.Head) %&gt;
&lt;%== PageDocument.Banner(PageDocument.Nav("Site", links, "site-nav")) %&gt;
&lt;%== PageDocument.OpenMain() %&gt;
&lt;%== layout.Body %&gt;
&lt;%== PageDocument.CloseMain() %&gt;
&lt;%== PageDocument.End() %&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The seeded <code>/_layout</code> is the full version of that page, and you can edit or restore it
from the page list.</p>

<h2 id="head-and-scripts">Stylesheets, head and scripts</h2>
<p>The page's key chooses its initial content type from the host's extension map; an unmapped key is
HTML. <code>ctx.SetContentType("text/css; charset=utf-8")</code> overrides it when a page needs to
send something else.</p>
<p>A page that loads a script calls <code>ctx.AllowScripts()</code>. That widens the response from
<code>script-src 'none'</code> to <code>script-src 'self'</code>; it does not allow inline scripts or
event-handler attributes:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% ctx.AllowScripts(); %&gt;
&lt;script type="module" src="/my-script.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The script itself is another stored page, or a seeded asset, served with a JavaScript content
type. Write it as a native ES module: use <code>import</code>/<code>export</code> rather than globals,
and end a side-effect-only script with <code>export {};</code>. Modules are already deferred, so the
script's position in the document does not matter.</p>

<h2 id="syntax">Template syntax</h2>
<p>Literal template text is HTML. A code block is C#, and the handler is asynchronous, so
<code>await</code> is an ordinary expression in a block and inside an output expression.</p>
<dl>
    <dt><code>&lt;% %&gt;</code></dt>
    <dd>C# statements. They can write with <code>ctx.Write</code> or <code>ctx.WriteRaw</code>.</dd>
    <dt><code>&lt;%= %&gt;</code></dt>
    <dd>Writes an HTML-escaped string, like <code>ctx.Write</code>.</dd>
    <dt><code>&lt;%== %&gt;</code></dt>
    <dd>Writes raw HTML, like <code>ctx.WriteRaw</code>. Only use it for markup you built or trust.</dd>
    <dt><code>&lt;%! %&gt;</code></dt>
    <dd>Declarations, such as <code>using</code> directives and types of the page's own. They are
    hoisted above the handler and keep their template order.</dd>
</dl>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You asked for &lt;%= ctx.Path %&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;% for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) { %&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Line &lt;%= i.ToString() %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;% } %&gt;</code></pre>
<p>An output expression must produce a string, so convert other values explicitly with
<code>.ToString()</code>.</p>
<p>A page is compiled with nullable analysis on, exactly as the host's own code is, so a reference
that may be null says so. <code>ctx.User</code> is a <code>PageUser?</code>, and
<code>ctx.Features.Get&lt;T&gt;()</code> returns <code>null</code> when this render does not offer
the capability. Bind a guard to a local and use the local, which is the form the compiler can
follow:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% if (ctx.User is not { } user) { ctx.RedirectToLogin(); return; } %&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Signed in as &lt;%= user.Name %&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
<p>A warning does not stop the page: it compiles, is stored and is served. It is still unfinished
work, the editor shows it beside your template, and the page list says <em>Compiled with
warnings</em> until it is fixed. A page that wants the old behaviour can write
<code>#nullable disable</code> in its own code.</p>
<p>A declaration block looks like this:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;%!
using System.Globalization;

sealed class Amount {
    public static string Format(decimal value) =&gt; value.ToString("N2", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
}
%&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Total: &lt;% ctx.Write(Amount.Format(42.5m)); %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Forms use <code>&lt;form class="form"&gt;</code> with one <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>; a label is a
<code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> and its control is the matching <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code>. A textarea goes in a
<code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> whose <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> contains its label.</p>

<h2 id="context">Context and capabilities</h2>
<p>The variable <code>ctx</code> implements <code>IContext</code>. It carries the request and the
response, and nothing else:</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>ctx.Request</code> is a <code>PageRequest</code> with read-only <code>Query</code>,
    <code>Form</code>, <code>Headers</code> and <code>Cookies</code> dictionaries.</li>
    <li><code>ctx.Path</code> is the page's canonical key; <code>ctx.User</code> is the signed-in
    <code>PageUser</code> or <code>null</code>; <code>ctx.Aborted</code> is cancelled when the
    visitor abandons the request.</li>
    <li><code>ctx.Log</code>, <code>ctx.LogWarning</code> and <code>ctx.LogError</code> write to the
    page log, tagged with this page's key.</li>
    <li><code>ctx.SetStatus</code>, <code>ctx.SetContentType</code>, <code>ctx.SetHeader</code>,
    <code>ctx.Redirect</code> and <code>ctx.RedirectToLogin</code> shape the response.</li>
    <li><code>ctx.AllowScripts</code> permits same-origin external scripts;
    <code>ctx.AntiforgeryField</code> carries the hidden input a form must include.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>ctx.Features</code> is how a page asks for an optional capability.
<code>TryGet&lt;T&gt;(out T?)</code> reports whether it is there, <code>Get&lt;T&gt;()</code> returns
<code>null</code> when it is not, and <code>GetRequired&lt;T&gt;()</code> throws naming the
interface. The host decides what is offered.</p>
<p><code>IBuildInfo</code> says which build is answering. <code>Commit</code> is the full git commit
hash, or <code>null</code> when the build did not come from a checkout:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% if (ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IBuildInfo&gt;().Commit is { } commit) { %&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/Moosemorals/uk.osric.asp/commit/&lt;%= commit %&gt;"&gt;&lt;%= commit %&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;% } %&gt;</code></pre>
<p><code>IPageStorage</code> is the page's own key/value store. Every visitor shares it, a write is
last-write-wins, and a page cannot reach another page's keys:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% IPageStorage storage = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IPageStorage&gt;();
   PageValue&lt;int&gt; stored = await storage.TryGetValueAsync&lt;int&gt;("visits");
   int visits = (stored.Found ? stored.Value : 0) + 1;
   await storage.SetAsync("visits", visits); %&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Visited &lt;%= visits.ToString() %&gt; times.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
<p><code>IHttpFetch</code> makes one bounded HTTP request from the host, not the browser, and only to
hosts the operator allow-lists. Failures are values rather than exceptions:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% IHttpFetch fetch = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IHttpFetch&gt;();
   HttpFetchResponse response = await fetch.GetAsync("https://api.example.com/status");
   if (response.Succeeded) { %&gt;
&lt;p&gt;State: &lt;%= response.Body %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;% } else { %&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unavailable (&lt;%= response.Failure.ToString() %&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;% } %&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The page API allows the <code>uk.osric.asp.Sdk</code> contract, the common collection, culture and
LINQ helpers, the explicit base types the contract uses, and the system exception types a page may
catch. <code>System.IO</code>, <code>System.Net</code>, <code>System.Reflection</code>,
<code>System.Diagnostics</code>, regular expressions, expression trees, interop, threading beyond
<code>CancellationToken</code>, <code>Microsoft.*</code> and host namespaces are not available.</p>

<h2 id="catalog">Reading the page store</h2>
<p><code>IPageCatalog</code> is a read-only view of the site's page store: list the heads under a
prefix, read a live page's template, list a page's history, and read one retained revision by its
key and hash. It never meets an image, a symbol or a write; saving is a separate capability.</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% IPageCatalog catalog = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IPageCatalog&gt;();
   System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList&lt;PageEntry&gt; pages = await catalog.ListAsync("/blog");
   PageSource? page = await catalog.ReadAsync("/blog");
   System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList&lt;PageRevisionInfo&gt; history = await catalog.RevisionsAsync("/blog"); %&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;% foreach (PageEntry entry in pages) { %&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= entry.Key %&gt; — &lt;%= entry.IsDeleted ? "deleted" : "live" %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;% } %&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
<p>A prefix is a subtree, not a string prefix: <code>"/blog"</code> lists <code>/blog</code> and
<code>/blog/post</code>, never <code>/blogroll</code>. The listing returns every head the store
holds — live pages, tombstones, seeded assets and reserved keys — each marked with
<code>IsDeleted</code>, <code>IsReserved</code> and <code>IsHtml</code>, so a view filters rather than
re-deriving the host's rules. A tombstone has no source: it is listed as deleted and reads as absent,
while its history stays readable.</p>
<p><strong>The read is as public as the page that calls it.</strong> There is no gate in the host: an
anonymous visitor gets the same answers a signed-in one does. A page that wants its listing or its
source private writes the ordinary guard at the top of its own source, exactly as any private page
does:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% if (ctx.User is not { } user) { ctx.RedirectToLogin(); return; } %&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The trade is worth stating plainly: a page that calls <code>ReadAsync</code> or
<code>ReadRevisionAsync</code> publishes the source it reads to whoever can reach that page, including
a template whose own guard hides its output, and including the history of a deleted page until it is
scrubbed. The seeded <a href="/recent">/recent</a> is a public listing and the seeded
<a href="/pages">/pages</a> is a guarded source and history view: the same capability, with and
without that one line. Both are demonstrations of this capability and of the editor's cheap half
(IDEA-017), not the editor itself.</p>
<p>The read is bounded. One listing returns at most 500 rows and refuses rather than truncating, and
one render may pull in at most 512 KiB of template across the page and its layouts, which is also a
refusal rather than half a page. A listing charges nothing, because its rows are metadata and the
body cap bounds what a page can write out of them. Both limits are set under
<code>Pages:Read</code>.</p>

<h2 id="markdown">Markdown</h2>
<p><code>IMarkdownRenderer</code> renders Markdown to HTML. Write its result raw:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% IMarkdownRenderer markdown = ctx.Features.GetRequired&lt;IMarkdownRenderer&gt;(); %&gt;
&lt;%== markdown.ToHtml("## Today\n\n- one\n- two") %&gt;</code></pre>
<p>It understands CommonMark, pipe tables, auto-links, links and images. Raw HTML in the
Markdown passes through, exactly like <code>&lt;%== %&gt;</code>: the renderer is not a
sanitiser, so escape a visitor's or a fetched value with <code>ctx.Write</code> rather than
rendering it. An image must be served by this site, because the response's policy allows images
only from the same origin. A table's header cells are written with <code>scope="col"</code>, and
column alignment markers are ignored because the site's policy forbids inline styles.</p>

<h2 id="private">Private pages and forms</h2>
<p>Pages are public unless their own code checks <code>ctx.User</code>. Put the guard at the top and
return after redirecting:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;% if (ctx.User is not { } user) { ctx.RedirectToLogin(); return; } %&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Draft&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Signed in as &lt;%= user.Name %&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Every form an authenticated visitor can submit must carry the antiforgery field written raw,
because the host refuses an authenticated <code>POST</code> without that pair:</p>
<pre class="source" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;form method="post" class="form"&gt;
    &lt;%== ctx.AntiforgeryField %&gt;
    &lt;dl&gt;
        &lt;dt&gt;&lt;label for="note"&gt;Note&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/dt&gt;
        &lt;dd&gt;&lt;input id="note" name="note"&gt;&lt;/dd&gt;
    &lt;/dl&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Do not write a sign-in form, handle a password or read the authentication cookie:
<code>/login</code> and <code>/logout</code> already exist and are reserved. A response rendered for
a signed-in visitor is sent <code>no-store</code> and without an <code>ETag</code>.</p>

<h2 id="assets">Assets, seeds and recovery</h2>
<p>A page whose key has a known extension is served with that content type, so stylesheets, scripts
and other assets are pages like any other. The host maps <code>.js</code>, <code>.mjs</code>,
<code>.css</code>, <code>.json</code> and <code>.txt</code> by default; an unmapped extension, such
as <code>.svg</code>, needs <code>ctx.SetContentType</code> on the page that serves it.</p>
<p>The pages the site ships with are called seeds. They live under <code>Pages/Seeds</code> and are
created only when the page does not already exist, so editing a seed changes the site without being
overwritten on the next start. The page list offers <strong>Restore from seed</strong> on a seeded
row, which saves the bundled source as a new revision; that is how you recover
<code>/_layout</code>, <code>/site.css</code> or this page after a bad edit.</p>
<p>Deleting a page leaves a tombstone and its history. Saving the path again continues that history.
Scrubbing removes the retained history, and it cannot undo copies that already left the database.</p>

<h2 id="keeping-current">Keeping this page current</h2>
<p>This page is the seeded page <code>/help</code>. Its source is
<code>src/uk.osric.asp.Web/Pages/Seeds/help</code>; use <a href="/edit/help">/edit/help</a> to change
it.</p>
<p>A change that adds or alters what a page author can do should update this page in the same change:
a new <code>IContext</code> member, capability, layout slot, editor action, content type or response
rule belongs here, next to the tests and the architecture notes. The plan for the change should say
so.</p>
